---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Services"
	description="AI strategy and governance, private AI, and implementation for regulated businesses, from Ohana Tech."
>
	<Nav />

	<div class="services-page">

		<div style="margin-bottom: 3rem; padding-bottom: 3rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
			<p class="section-label">Services</p>
			<h1 style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.75rem); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.025em; margin: 0 0 1rem;">
				What we deliver
			</h1>
			<p style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 62ch; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0;">
				Most engagements follow the same arc: understand where AI fits and how it will be governed, decide
				where it should run, then put it into production. Each service stands on its own, and each builds
				on the one before it.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Strategy & Governance -->
		<div class="service-area" id="strategy">
			<p class="section-label">Service 01</p>
			<h2>AI Strategy & Governance</h2>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				Most organizations already have people using AI, with or without a policy. A discovery engagement
				establishes where AI can help, where it shouldn't be used, and what controls need to be in place
				before it scales. We interview leadership and department heads, map the workflows that matter,
				and turn what we learn into decisions your team can act on.
			</p>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				Governance here is practical: an AI use policy your people can follow, clear ownership for AI-assisted
				decisions, and a record of what each AI system was allowed to know and do. We work alongside your
				legal and compliance function, which continues to own regulatory interpretation.
			</p>
			<ul class="service-detail-list">
				<li>Current-state assessment and AI opportunity map by department</li>
				<li>Company AI use policy</li>
				<li>Governance framework and maturity assessment</li>
				<li>Risk-tiered workflows: what AI may draft, decide, or never touch</li>
				<li>Target-state architecture recommendation</li>
				<li>Phased implementation roadmap and cost estimate</li>
				<li>Executive readout</li>
				<li>Alignment with NIST AI RMF, the EU AI Act, and your industry's quality standards</li>
			</ul>
		</div>

		<!-- Private AI -->
		<div class="service-area" id="private-ai">
			<p class="section-label">Service 02</p>
			<h2>Private AI</h2>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				For many regulated businesses, the question isn't whether AI is useful. It's whether design files,
				customer records, or financial data can be sent to a public AI service at all. Private AI keeps the
				models and the data inside your security boundary, running open-weight models on dedicated
				infrastructure.
			</p>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				It also changes the economics. Capacity-based infrastructure has a cost you can forecast, rather than
				a metered bill that grows with every new user. And because the models are open-weight, you aren't
				tied to a single vendor's pricing or product decisions.
			</p>
			<ul class="service-detail-list">
				<li>Private by default: data stays inside your boundary</li>
				<li>Policy-based routing: sensitive data goes only to private models, by rule rather than by habit</li>
				<li>Governance built in: human review points, audit trail, and provenance</li>
				<li>Integration with your identity, access control, and security tooling</li>
				<li>Chat and developer tools your people will actually use</li>
				<li>Scales with usage without re-architecture</li>
				<li>Predictable, capacity-based cost</li>
			</ul>
		</div>

		<!-- Implementation -->
		<div class="service-area" id="implementation">
			<p class="section-label">Service 03</p>
			<h2>Implementation</h2>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				A roadmap only matters once it's running. We deliver pilots first, measure them against the outcomes
				the roadmap set, and expand what works. Adoption is planned alongside the technology, so governance
				keeps pace with use rather than trailing behind it.
			</p>
			<ul class="service-detail-list">
				<li>Pilot selection, delivery, and measurement</li>
				<li>Integration with existing systems and data sources</li>
				<li>Agentic workflows with defined boundaries and oversight</li>
				<li>Access control, audit, and monitoring in production</li>
				<li>Training and competency for the people using AI day to day</li>
				<li>Ongoing governance review as models and regulations change</li>
			</ul>
		</div>

		<!-- Technology -->
		<div style="margin: 3rem 0 2rem; padding-top: 3rem; border-top: 2px solid var(--color-border);">
			<p class="section-label">The technology underneath</p>
			<h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.5rem); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.015em; margin: 0 0 1rem;">
				Open foundations for governed AI
			</h2>
			<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 62ch; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0;">
				Our services are built on technology we develop in the open, so governance is something you can
				inspect rather than take on trust.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="service-area" id="scs">
			<h2>Structured Context Specification</h2>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				An open specification (Apache 2.0) for machine-readable governance context. SCS turns policies,
				standards, and decisions into versioned, reviewable artifacts that AI systems can use and auditors
				can check.
			</p>
			<div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
				<a href="https://structuredcontext.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-secondary">structuredcontext.dev</a>
				<a href="https://github.com/tim-mccrimmon/structured-context-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-secondary">GitHub &rarr;</a>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="service-area" id="scp">
			<h2>Supervisory Control Plane</h2>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				The runtime that delivers governance context to AI agents and records what each agent knew when it
				acted: agent identity, policies delivered, and context version, for every request.
			</p>
			<div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
				<a href="https://supervisoryplane.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-secondary">supervisoryplane.com</a>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="service-area" id="paepae">
			<h2>paepae</h2>
			<p class="service-area-intro">
				A runtime for agentic workflows. paepae manages the context of multi-agent applications as they run
				and reports into the control plane, so agent-to-agent work stays inside the same governance as
				everything else.
			</p>
		</div>

	</div>

	<!-- CTA banner -->
	<div class="cta-banner">
		<div class="cta-banner-inner">
			<div>
				<h2>Not sure where to start?</h2>
				<p>A discovery engagement is usually the right first step. Tell us about your business and we'll suggest a scope.</p>
			</div>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Get in Touch</a>
		</div>
	</div>

	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
